  "title": "The Spectrum of Consciousness",
  "emoji": "💭",
  "color": "#581C87",
  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: The Spectrum of Consciousness.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Consciousness is not a light switch. It is more like a dimmer that slides between deep coma and vivid, focused awareness.",
      "visual": "🎚️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Above is coma, where the brain has some activity but no wakefulness.",
      "visual": "📉"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The vegetative state preserves sleep-wake cycles but lacks awareness.",
      "visual": "🌗"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Normal waking consciousness itself varies: drowsy, alert, hyper-focused, or in flow states where self-awareness temporarily.",
      "visual": "🌊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The Glasgow Coma Scale rates consciousness on a 3-to-15 point spectrum, proving clinicians treat it as a continuum, not a binary.",
      "visual": "📏"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You are never simply conscious or not.",
      "visual": "🌅"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What does the minimally conscious state involve?",
    "options": [
      "Inconsistent but real signs of awareness",
      "Complete absence of all brain activity",
      "Full awareness with inability to move"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
